.TH QFW-SLURM 7 "September 2026" "qfw-slurm 0.1.0" "Miscellaneous Information"
.SH NAME
qfw-slurm \- Slurm allocation integration for QFw QPM reservations
.SH OVERVIEW
qfw-slurm maps Slurm allocation lifecycle events to QFw QPM reservations. A
required SPANK module collects bounded workload requirements before the first
managed task. A persistent gateway authenticates the request, verifies the job
with slurmctld, discovers exact QPM services through DEFw, and calls their
reservation APIs. A controller epilog releases the reservation set after the
allocation terminates.
.PP
The standalone
.BR qfw-slurm-driver (1)
uses the same native client and operation layer without loading SPANK. It is a
diagnostic tool and does not validate callback placement or environment
mutation.
.SH COMPONENTS
.TP
.B spank_quantum.so
Registers Slurm options, constructs one allocation request, denies launch on
failure, and exports accepted reservation tuples.
.TP
.BR qfw-slurm-gateway (8)
Serves authenticated QSGP requests and owns the durable SQLite journal.
.TP
.BR qfw-slurm-epilog (8)
Performs allocation-level release from
.BR EpilogSlurmctld .
.TP
.BR qfw-slurm-driver (1)
Exercises reserve, release, or both for explicit active-job metadata.
.SH SLURM OPTIONS
The first managed step supplies the selected QPM resources and workload bounds.
.TP
.BI --qpu = name[,name...]
Select configured QPU resource names. Each maps to one exact stable QPM service
ID.
.TP
.BI --workload-kind = kind
Select
.B quantum
or
.BR hybrid .
.TP
.BI --circ-count = count
Set the maximum circuit count.
.TP
.BI --max-qubits = count
Set the maximum qubits per circuit.
.TP
.BI --max-depth = count
Set the maximum circuit depth.
.TP
.BI --max-shots = count
Set the maximum shots.
.TP
.BI --max-one-q-gates = count
Set the optional one-qubit gate bound.
.TP
.BI --max-two-q-gates = count
Set the optional two-qubit gate bound.
.TP
.BI --max-measurements = count
Set the optional measurement bound.
.SH RESERVATION ENVIRONMENT
After acceptance, the plugin installs
.B QFW_RESERVATIONS
in the task environment. Its canonical JSON value is an array of unique
service-ID and reservation-ID tuples:
.PP
.RS
.B [["nwqsim-site","41"],["iqm-ornl-20q","17"]]
.RE
.PP
Reservation IDs are decimal strings. A later managed step requesting the same
service set receives the stored tuple set without creating another QPM
reservation. A conflicting service set is rejected.
.SH LIFECYCLE
The gateway treats reserve as an allocation-level atomic operation. If one QPM
rejects or fails, the gateway releases every reservation already accepted by
that request before returning failure.
.PP
Release is exhaustive. Every QPM result is visited even when another service
is unavailable. Terminal releases are recorded as
.BR released .
Authorization, QPM, or gateway failures produce
.B release-incomplete
state and remain available for operator retry.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
QSGP frames are encoded in network byte order and protected by MUNGE. The
gateway verifies both the MUNGE sender and authoritative Slurm job identity.
It never receives application circuits or provider credentials.
.SH VALIDATION BOUNDARIES
Native tests cover protocol, client, and operation behavior. The deterministic
gateway suite covers synchronous request and journal behavior without a live
QPM. Driver testing against a production gateway covers DEFw and QPM calls.
Only an actual
.B srun
test covers SPANK callback placement, option propagation, and
.BR spank_setenv (3).
